  "Number": 39,
  "Question": "We may say concerning the solution of \\(|x|^2+|x|-6=0\\) that:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "there is only one root",
    "B": "the sum of the roots is \\(1\\)",
    "C": "the sum of the roots is \\(0\\)",
    "D": "the product of the roots is \\(4\\)",
    "E": "the product of the roots is \\(-6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Both variable terms involve \\(|x|\\), so let \\(u=|x|\\), remembering that \\(u\\ge0\\). The equation becomes\n\\[\nu^2+u-6=(u+3)(u-2)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(u=-3\\) or \\(u=2\\). An absolute value cannot be negative, so only \\(u=2\\) is allowed.\n\nThe equation \\(|x|=2\\) gives exactly \\(x=-2\\) and \\(x=2\\). Both satisfy the original equation because \\(2^2+2-6=0\\). Their sum is\n\\[\n-2+2=\\boxed{0},\n\\]\nso choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\) is correct.",
